From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 09:10:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 38/38] vc4gfx: read the HVS registers in the byte order the
 HVS uses

The takeover has been declining on every Pi 3 boot with

    [VC4HVS] takeover: HDMI channel not usable (CTRL=38047880 head=0)

and the numbers are the whole story. 0x38047880 is 0x80780438 with its
bytes reversed, and 0x80780438 is exactly ENABLE | 1920 << 12 | 1080 --
the channel was enabled and running the mode we expected. The same holds
for every other register the driver read: HVS_ID came back 0x76726464
where the header records 0x64647276, and a later dump reported a
display-list head of 0x64060000, which is word 0x664, a perfectly
ordinary index into the 4096-word dlist RAM.

hvs_rd() and pv_rd() dereference a volatile ULONG * and hand the result
straight back. On arm-native that is right, because the CPU and the
peripheral agree. On m68k they do not: the registers are little-endian
like every other BCM283x peripheral, so each read arrives byte-reversed
and the takeover judged a healthy channel to be disabled with an
out-of-range list.

The rest of this driver already knew that -- vcgfx_dma.c wraps the DMA
control registers and the system timer in AROS_LE2LONG/AROS_LONG2LE, and
so does this port's mbox.resource. The four HVS/PixelValve accessors were
the ones written for a little-endian host and never revisited. Wrapping
them costs nothing where the host is little-endian: AROS_LE2LONG compiles
away and the load is the same instruction it was.

Second change, in the same failure path: the fb-plane search only
recognised RGBA8888. The surface this port is handed is RGB565 -- 16-bit,
pitch 3840 for 1920 pixels -- so even reading the list correctly the
takeover would have walked past its own framebuffer and reported "no
usable fb plane". The word layout, which is what the size check tests, is
the same for both; the depth only tells the HVS how to sample. PTR0 ==
fb_phys remains the identification that matters.

Neither half is verified on hardware yet: what is verified is that the
values the driver printed cannot be read any other way.
diff --git a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
index 209c3250d6..6e44af7e8d 100644
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
@@ -62,24 +62,37 @@ static inline BOOL hvs_hw_known(struct VideoCoreGfx_staticdata *xsd)
     return !xsd->vcsd_IsBCM2711;
 }
 
+/*
+ * HVS and PixelValve registers are little-endian, like every other BCM283x
+ * peripheral -- vcgfx_dma.c already says so for the DMA engine and the
+ * system timer. On a little-endian CPU the conversion is nothing and the
+ * generated code is the same bare load; on a big-endian one it is the
+ * difference between reading a register and reading its bytes backwards.
+ *
+ * That difference is not academic here. On m68k the raw reads returned
+ * HVS_ID = 0x76726464 (the header records 0x64647276), DISPCTRLX1 =
+ * 0x38047880 (the real 0x80780438 = ENABLE | 1920 << 12 | 1080) and a
+ * display-list head of 0x64060000 for word 0x664 -- so the takeover saw a
+ * disabled channel with an out-of-range list and declined every time.
+ */
 static inline ULONG hvs_rd(ULONG offset)
 {
-    return *(volatile ULONG *)(VC4_HVS_BASE + offset);
+    return AROS_LE2LONG(*(volatile ULONG *)(VC4_HVS_BASE + offset));
 }
 
 static inline void hvs_wr(ULONG offset, ULONG value)
 {
-    *(volatile ULONG *)(VC4_HVS_BASE + offset) = value;
+    *(volatile ULONG *)(VC4_HVS_BASE + offset) = AROS_LONG2LE(value);
 }
 
 static inline ULONG pv_rd(IPTR base, ULONG offset)
 {
-    return *(volatile ULONG *)(base + offset);
+    return AROS_LE2LONG(*(volatile ULONG *)(base + offset));
 }
 
 static inline void pv_wr(IPTR base, ULONG offset, ULONG value)
 {
-    *(volatile ULONG *)(base + offset) = value;
+    *(volatile ULONG *)(base + offset) = AROS_LONG2LE(value);
 }
 
 static const char * const hvs_fmt_names[] =
@@ -688,9 +701,17 @@ BOOL vc4_hvs_takeover(struct VideoCoreGfx_staticdata *xsd,
                 break;
 
             /* Recognize the fb plane by its PTR0. Two known layouts,
-             * both single-plane RGBA: unity (7 words, ptr at +4) and
-             * scaled (14 words, ptr at +5, POS1 = dest size at +2). */
-            if ((ctl0 & HVS_CTL0_FORMAT_MASK) == HVS_PXF_RGBA8888)
+             * both single-plane: unity (7 words, ptr at +4) and scaled
+             * (14 words, ptr at +5, POS1 = dest size at +2). The word
+             * layout is what the size check tests; the depth only picks
+             * how the HVS samples the surface, and both depths this port
+             * can be handed are single-plane packed RGB. RGB565 is the
+             * one the m68k-emu68 bootloader sets up (16-bit surface,
+             * pitch = 2 * width), so a takeover that only knew RGBA8888
+             * could never inherit anything here. PTR0 == fb_phys stays
+             * the real identification. */
+            if ((ctl0 & HVS_CTL0_FORMAT_MASK) == HVS_PXF_RGBA8888
+                || (ctl0 & HVS_CTL0_FORMAT_MASK) == HVS_PXF_RGB565)
             {
                 ULONG ptroff = 0;
 
